# graphlaw_ex: admission through the wasm turnstile from BEAM

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:graphlaw_ex, path: __DIR__},
    {:wasmex, "~> 0.15.1"},
    {:jason, "~> 1.4"},
    {:telemetry, "~> 1.2"}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

    18:31:59.436 [debug] Copying NIF from cache and extracting to .../libwasmex-v0.15.1-nif-2.15-aarch64-apple-darwin.so

## The idea

`graphlaw_ex` is the BEAM bridge over the graphlaw WASM law kernel. The BEAM side holds only
the wasm byte-slice; the bridge owns bytes in and out over the module's ABI version 1
(`gl_alloc` / `gl_call` / `gl_free`); the host owns authority, standing and refusals. Nothing on
the Elixir side interprets RDF, SHACL, SQL or receipts — law admission happens *inside* the
module (the deterministic turnstile boundary).

```text
BEAM caller
    |
    v
GraphlawEx (GenServer) ---> packet-4 frame ---> wasm module (digest-pinned artifact)
    |                                              |
    v                                              v
{:ok, response_map}  <--- ok: true          {:error, {:refused, error_map}}
                            (typed refusal raised inside the module)
```

This notebook installs the checkout you opened it from (`path: __DIR__`). Every output below is
produced when you evaluate the cell; none is pre-filled.

## The pinned artifact

The engine is a pinned artifact, not a dependency. The bridge refuses to start on a missing
file and answers with a typed error instead of a linked-process exit. The pin lives in
`priv/graphlaw.wasm.blake3`; verify the bytes you are about to load against it before starting
the bridge:

```bash
b3sum ../priv/graphlaw.wasm   # from this notebook's directory (graphlaw_ex/)
```

    c38b1fdb97ba462dc96000301d7ddf6a5109a7208c68a52f5ddd63ccbd6fda2c  priv/graphlaw.wasm

Any other digest means the bytes are not the pinned artifact — do not admit through them.

## Start the bridge

The application starts no engine by default (`:auto_start` is opt-in), so depending on the
library never instantiates wasm implicitly. Start the bridge yourself against the pinned
artifact:

```elixir
wasm_path = Path.expand("../priv/graphlaw.wasm", __DIR__)

case GraphlawEx.start_link(wasm_path: wasm_path) do
  {:ok, _bridge} -> :bridge_started
  {:error, {:already_started, _}} -> :already_running
end
```

    :bridge_started

A missing artifact is a typed failure returned from `start_link/1`, never a crash:

```elixir
case GraphlawEx.start_link(wasm_path: "/nonexistent/graphlaw.wasm") do
  {:ok, _} -> {:unexpected, :started}
  {:error, {:wasm_load_failed, path, :enoent}} -> {:refused_load, path, :enoent}
end
```

    {:refused_load, "/nonexistent/graphlaw.wasm", :enoent}

## An honest admit

One real round trip through the ABI: an RDFS entailment law runs *inside* the module and the
receipt JSON comes back through `gl_alloc` / `gl_call` / `gl_free`.

```elixir
honest = %{
  "op" => "law",
  "data" => %{
    "text" => "@prefix : <https://test.graphlaw.dev/> .\n:a :edge :b .\n:b :edge :c .\n",
    "dialect" => "turtle"
  },
  "steps" => [%{"step" => "rdfs"}]
}

case GraphlawEx.admit(Jason.encode!(honest)) do
  {:ok, %{"ok" => true} = response} ->
    {:admitted, nquads_bytes: byte_size(response["nquads"]), states: length(response["states"]),
      receipts: length(response["receipts"])}

  {:error, reason} ->
    {:refused, reason}
end
```

    {:admitted, nquads_bytes: 18113, states: 2, receipts: 1}

The entailment (`:a :edge :c` follows from the transitive closure) was computed inside the wasm
module; BEAM only moved bytes.

## A tampered refuse

An unknown op is refused *inside the module* — the kernel raises a typed refusal in the answer
payload and the bridge surfaces it as `{:error, {:refused, error}}`:

```elixir
GraphlawEx.admit(Jason.encode!(%{"op" => "definitely-not-an-op"}))
```

    {:error,
     {:refused,
      %{
        "dialect" => nil,
        "engine" => nil,
        "kind" => "Unsupported",
        "message" => "unknown op `definitely-not-an-op`"
      }}}

Nothing was guessed on the host side: the refusal `kind`, `message` and closed structure are the
module's own answer, relayed verbatim.

## Telemetry

Every admit emits a telemetry span on `[:graphlaw_ex, :admit, :stop]`. Attach a handler, make
one call, read what the span reported:

```elixir
parent = self()
handler_id = "livebook-admit-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

:telemetry.attach(
  handler_id,
  [:graphlaw_ex, :admit, :stop],
  fn _event, _measurements, metadata, _config -> send(parent, {:span, metadata}) end,
  nil
)

_ = GraphlawEx.admit(Jason.encode!(honest))
:telemetry.detach(handler_id)

receive do
  {:span, metadata} -> {:span, metadata}
after
  1_000 -> :no_span_received
end
```

    :no_span_received

Status note (as of this build): the `[:graphlaw_ex, :admit, :stop]` span is the event name the
`lib/` telemetry surface defines; this build does not emit it yet, so the honest output of this
cell on the current tree is `:no_span_received` — a loud absence, not a faked pass. When the
span lands, the same cell returns `{:span, metadata}`.

